Q: how do you pass the environment variables on docker build command.
ANS : 
There are three main ways to pass environment variables when building Docker images. Each serves a different purpose
depending on whether you want the variable only during build or included in the final image
  1. 🟢 1️⃣ Using --build-arg (Recommended for Build-Time Variables)
🔸 Dockerfile:
        ARG APP_ENV
        RUN echo "Building for environment: $APP_ENV"
🔸 Command:
docker build --build-arg APP_ENV=production -t my-image .
---------------------------------------
2. 🟢 2️⃣ Using ENV (Sets Variable in the Final Image)
Use ENV in the Dockerfile to make variables available at runtime in containers.
🔸 Dockerfile:
ENV APP_ENV=production
✅ This value will be available inside the container when it runs:
  -> docker run my-image env
🟢 3️⃣ Combine ARG + ENV for Dynamic Runtime Value
dockerfile

ARG APP_ENV
ENV APP_ENV=${APP_ENV}
    -> docker build --build-arg APP_ENV=staging -t my-app .
✅ The variable is available both during build and in the final image.
